<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nur Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amanus Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nur Mountains, the ancient Amanus, are a range in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> running north-east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast near İskenderun and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Their passes, the Syrian and Amanian Gates, carried the main routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the kingdom of Sam'al lay in the valley to their east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nur-mountains.jpg" alt="Nur Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Radyokid at Turkish Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Nur_Mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Nur Mountains, in Turkish Nur Dağları, meaning Mountains of Holy Light, are a range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatay-province">Hatay Province</a> in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. They were formerly called Alma-Dağ, were the ancient Mount Amanus, were known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as the Black Mountain and are called Jabal al-Lukkam in Arabic. The range begins south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> and the Ceyhan river, runs roughly parallel to the Gulf of İskenderun and ends on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast between the gulf and the mouth of the Orontes.</p><p>It is about 200 kilometres long and rises to 2,240 metres, and it divides the coastal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, forming a natural border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the rest of West Asia. The Belen Pass, the Syrian Gates, lies near the town of Belen, and the Amanic Gates farther north. The Amanos Mountains are recognised as a biodiversity hotspot and as one of nine environmental hotspots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, with typical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> flora and many endemic species.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Monks had lived solitary lives in the mountains since the fourth century, and in the 10th and 11th centuries the Black Mountain became a major centre of Byzantine monasticism, comparable for the Byzantine East to Athos in the West. Armenian, Melkite, Jacobite, Georgian and Catholic monasteries and hermits were established there, and the Turks called the range Gâvur Dağ, Mount of Infidels. In 1028 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iii-argyros">Romanos III</a>, troubled by the number of heretical monks, tried to draft them for his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><p>In 1066 the Seljuk general Afshin Bey devastated the monasteries and killed many monks, and in 1098 the monks gave provisions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> army besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. The monasteries were plundered again after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ager-sanguinis">Battle of Ager Sanguinis</a> in 1119, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> slew many monks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nur Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sam'al arose in the 10th century BCE in a valley east of the <b>Amanus</b> mountains, near modern Islahiye.</blockquote><a href="/state/samal">Sam'al</a></li><li><blockquote>Tarcondimotus, a local ruler in the <b>Amanus</b> mountains of eastern Cilicia, supported Pompey when Rome reorganised the region in 64 BCE and was recognised as dynast.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarcondimotid-cilicia">Tarcondimotid Cilicia</a></li></ul></section>
